c numbers.\n\n- **Per-character view:** One comparison reports that on Claude, Korean runs about 106 net tokens per 100 characters, against about 32 tokens per 100 characters for English. That is a per-character measure, not a per-meaning ratio, so it isn't directly comparable to the figures above. The same source notes that Claude Fable 5, Opus 4.8, and Sonnet 5 share one tokenizer, and that Claude is not the leanest tokenizer for Korean. A separate source says GPT-5.5 is leanest for Korean.\n\n**Bottom line:** Most credible estimates put Korean at roughly 1.3x to 1.4x the English token count for the same content, with informal claims of 2x to 3x. The range depends on the text type and the tokenizer, and I found no official Anthropic figure. To get a number for your own content, count tokens with Anthropic's token-counting tool or API on matched English and Korean text."],"durationSeconds":9.8672717,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PujMdpA7nB5npV9WQXsZcv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JoEufGSFazTjGPUj23cCNt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Korean vs English token count ratio GPT-4o o200k_base cl100k tokenizer\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024\",\"url\":\"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8\"},{\"title\":\"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured\",\"url\":\"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/\"},{\"title\":\"token counting\",\"url\":\"https://aischool.lillytechsystems.com/tiktokenizer/token-counting.html\"},{\"title\":\"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?\",\"url\":\"https://horadecodar.com.br/?p=43288\"},{\"title\":\"Fantastic GPT-40 but...where are the docs?\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/fantastic-gpt-40-but-where-are-the-docs/744371/4\"},{\"title\":\"# Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682.md\"},{\"title\":\"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?\",\"url\":\"https://horadecodar.com.br/tokens-portugues/\"},{\"title\":\"tokenization visualizer\",\"url\":\"https://blakecrosley.com/blog/tokenization-visualizer\"}]\n\nI found no authoritative head-to-head benchmark of Korean token counts under o200k_base versus cl100k_base. The sources that do give numbers disagree a lot, and most don't isolate the tokenizer as the variable.\n\n**Figures for o200k_base (GPT-4o)**\n- A small study measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with o200k_base and found Korean at about 1.30x English tokens on average, with a median of 1.33x and a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Technical paragraphs were worst at 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). The sample is tiny.\n- A blog post tokenized 285 bilingual articles with several tokenizers and reported that Korean ran 1.38x English tokens, Japanese 1.34x. The post doesn't clearly say which tokenizer produced that headline figure, so treat it cautiously.\n- In a single-sentence example, the same meaning took 12 tokens in English and 20 in Korean under o200k_base, about 1.7x, even though the Korean had fewer characters. Twelve tokens under OpenAI's o200k_base. Twenty tokens. Less than half the characters, but 1.7x the tokens.\n\n**Figures for cl100k_base (GPT-4)**\n- An academic paper (ICICPE 2024) used OpenAI's tiktoken for GPT-4 and the FLORES-200 parallel corpus. It found that tokenization of the Korean sentence resulted in nearly four times more tokens for one comparison. That is a per-sentence observation, not a corpus-wide ratio, and the paper does not appear to report o200k_base, so it can't be compared directly to the figures above.\n- A third blog post claims Every Korean translation costs approximately 2.8x the English equivalent. It doesn't say which tokenizer or how it was measured, so I wouldn't rely on it.\n\n**Direct comparison of the two tokenizers**\n- I did not find a source that measures Korean specifically under both tokenizers on the same corpus. One Portuguese-language article says o200k_base doubled the vocabulary relative to GPT-4's cl100k_base, going from 100 thousand to 200 thousand tokens. A larger vocabulary plausibly includes more Korean subwords, which would suggest o200k_base reduces the Korean penalty. But that is an inference, not a measured result in these sources.\n\n**Bottom line:** Under o200k_base, the best available estimate is roughly 1.3x to 1.4x English token count for Korean, with individual texts ranging from about 1.1x to 1.7x. The 2.8x and \"nearly 4x\" figures come from different methods and aren't directly comparable. To get a reliable ratio, run tiktoken with `o200k_base` and `cl100k_base` on your own parallel corpus, such as FLORES-200, and compare.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c1496c7-a380-4fcf-a5c2-7d2accf0fc54","uuid":"3449497d-0a1a-4907-9626-6df288d5ea0d","timestamp":"2026-10-18T10:47:28.251Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Korean vs English token count ratio GPT-4o o200k_base cl100k tokenizer","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QRyjzqHZYnyxBv498r4e7o","content":[{"title":"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024","url":"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf"},{"title":"DEV Community","url":"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8"},{"title":"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured","url":"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/"},{"title":"token counting","url":"https://aischool.lillytechsystems.com/tiktokenizer/token-counting.html"},{"title":"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?","url":"https://horadecodar.com.br/?p=43288"},{"title":"Fantastic GPT-40 but...where are the docs?","url":"https://community.openai.com/t/fantastic-gpt-40-but-where-are-the-docs/744371/4"},{"title":"# Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean","url":"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682.md"},{"title":"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?","url":"https://horadecodar.com.br/tokens-portugues/"},{"title":"tokenization visualizer","url":"https://blakecrosley.com/blog/tokenization-visualizer"}]},"I found no authoritative head-to-head benchmark of Korean token counts under o200k_base versus cl100k_base. The sources that do give numbers disagree a lot, and most don't isolate the tokenizer as the variable.\n\n**Figures for o200k_base (GPT-4o)**\n- A small study measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with o200k_base and found Korean at about 1.30x English tokens on average, with a median of 1.33x and a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Technical paragraphs were worst at 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). The sample is tiny.\n- A blog post tokenized 285 bilingual articles with several tokenizers and reported that Korean ran 1.38x English tokens, Japanese 1.34x. The post doesn't clearly say which tokenizer produced that headline figure, so treat it cautiously.\n- In a single-sentence example, the same meaning took 12 tokens in English and 20 in Korean under o200k_base, about 1.7x, even though the Korean had fewer characters. Twelve tokens under OpenAI's o200k_base. Twenty tokens. Less than half the characters, but 1.7x the tokens.\n\n**Figures for cl100k_base (GPT-4)**\n- An academic paper (ICICPE 2024) used OpenAI's tiktoken for GPT-4 and the FLORES-200 parallel corpus. It found that tokenization of the Korean sentence resulted in nearly four times more tokens for one comparison. That is a per-sentence observation, not a corpus-wide ratio, and the paper does not appear to report o200k_base, so it can't be compared directly to the figures above.\n- A third blog post claims Every Korean translation costs approximately 2.8x the English equivalent. It doesn't say which tokenizer or how it was measured, so I wouldn't rely on it.\n\n**Direct comparison of the two tokenizers**\n- I did not find a source that measures Korean specifically under both tokenizers on the same corpus. One Portuguese-language article says o200k_base doubled the vocabulary relative to GPT-4's cl100k_base, going from 100 thousand to 200 thousand tokens. A larger vocabulary plausibly includes more Korean subwords, which would suggest o200k_base reduces the Korean penalty. But that is an inference, not a measured result in these sources.\n\n**Bottom line:** Under o200k_base, the best available estimate is roughly 1.3x to 1.4x English token count for Korean, with individual texts ranging from about 1.1x to 1.7x. The 2.8x and \"nearly 4x\" figures come from different methods and aren't directly comparable. To get a reliable ratio, run tiktoken with `o200k_base` and `cl100k_base` on your own parallel corpus, such as FLORES-200, and compare."],"durationSeconds":10.5583045,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JoEufGSFazTjGPUj23cCNt","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WWHppKkPwewnYcLudNXhBB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Petrov \"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\" Korean premium\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/74bb24dca8334adce292883b4b651eda-Abstract.html\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.15425v1\"},{\"title\":\"Language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages\",\"url\":\"https://intelligent-earth.ox.ac.uk/node/4027061\"},{\"title\":\"neurips.cc\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2023/poster/72721\"},{\"title\":\"tokenization fairness\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/AleksandarPetrov/tokenization-fairness\"},{\"title\":\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\",\"url\":\"https://liner.com/review/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-unfairness-between-languages\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.15425v1\"},{\"title\":\"language model tokenizers introduce 1\",\"url\":\"https://sotaverified.org/papers/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-1\"},{\"title\":\"language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages  40c63e9e\",\"url\":\"https://object.cloud.sdsc.edu/v1/AUTH_da4962d3368042ac8337e2dfdd3e7bf3/ml-papers-txt/NeurIPS/2023/language_model_tokenizers_introduce_unfairness_between_languages__40c63e9e.txt\"}]\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024\",\"url\":\"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"All languages are NOT created (tokenized) equal\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/all-languages-are-not-created-tokenized-equal/216407\"},{\"title\":\"Why Your LLM Costs 5X More If You Don't Speak English\",\"url\":\"https://programmerraja.is-a.dev/post/2025/Why-Your-LLM-Costs-5X-More-If-You-Don't-Speak-English\"},{\"title\":\"# All languages are NOT created (tokenized) equal\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/all-languages-are-not-created-tokenized-equal/216407.md\"},{\"title\":\"# Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682.md\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.15425v1\"},{\"title\":\"26401 feature request non english users korean japanese cjk face structural disadvanta\",\"url\":\"https://claudeissues.com/issue/26401-feature-request-non-english-users-korean-japanese-cjk-face-structural-disadvanta\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8\"},{\"title\":\"language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages  40c63e9e\",\"url\":\"https://object.cloud.sdsc.edu/v1/AUTH_da4962d3368042ac8337e2dfdd3e7bf3/ml-papers-txt/NeurIPS/2023/language_model_tokenizers_introduce_unfairness_between_languages__40c63e9e.txt\"}]\n\nThe paper you're looking for is by Aleksandar Petrov, Emanuele La Malfa, Philip Torr, and Adel Bibi, published at NeurIPS 2023 and on arXiv (2305.15425). My searches did not find Korean-specific figures in the paper itself, so I can't confirm a \"Korean premium\" from it.\n\n**The paper**\n- It argues that disparity in the treatment of different languages arises at the tokenization stage, well before a model is even invoked.\n- It reports that the same text translated into different languages can have drastically different tokenization lengths, with differences up to 15 times in some cases.\n- It defines the \"premium\" as the ratio |t(s A)|/|t(s B)|, meaning the token count for one language divided by the count for a reference language.\n- Its examples come mostly from other languages. One passage notes that the tokenizer employed by Chat GPT and GPT-4 uses about 1.6 times more tokens to encode the same text in Italian as it does in English, 2.6 times for Bulgarian and 3 times for Arabic.\n- It also covers the fairness consequences: commercial services charge users per token or Unicode character, and these discrepancies lead to users of some languages paying at least 2.5 times more for the same task as users of English.\n\n**Korean-specific sources (not from the Petrov paper)**\n- A Korea University study titled \"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer\" reports that despite the shorter length of the Korean sentence compared to its English counterpart, tokenization of the Korean sentence resulted in nearly four times more tokens. This is based on a single example, so treat it as illustrative.\n- A community-posted comparison lists Korean at 1.6x the English token cost, with Japanese at 1.8x and Chinese at 2.0x. The source is a secondary blog-style summary and I could not verify its methodology.\n- A separate analysis measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x. This is a tokenizer-specific measurement, not the Petrov study.\n\nSo the Korean numbers vary a lot depending on tokenizer and test sentences, ranging from roughly 1.3x to about 4x in these sources. If you need the paper's exact Korean premium, check the full PDF's per-language tables on arXiv (2305.15425) or the GitHub repository (AleksandarPetrov/tokenization-fairness), which contains the tokenization length data.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c1496c7-a380-4fcf-a5c2-7d2accf0fc54","uuid":"e5e24c0d-92a0-46ed-9735-ec13296c3810","timestamp":"2026-10-18T10:47:29.158Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Petrov \"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\" Korean premium","results":[{"tool_use_id":"s