\n| Japanese | 1.34 | 1.64 | 1.57 |\n| Korean | **1.38** | **1.82** | **1.88** |\n\n**Other numbers on the page**\n- **Tokens per character:** English 0.235, Korean 0.408, Chinese 0.419, Japanese 0.471. The page doesn't say which tokenizer these figures use.\n- **Token drop from cl100k to o200k:** English 0.7%, Chinese 17.4%, Japanese 19.0%, Korean 24.7%.\n- **Per-article ratios on o200k only:**\n\n  | Language | Median | Mean |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Chinese | 1.10 | 1.14 |\n  | Japanese | 1.41 | 1.40 |\n  | Korean | 1.31 | 1.40 |\n\n  Posts heavy in code blocks came out near 1.1.\n- **One test sentence** (\"I refactored the agent loop to cut token usage.\") in o200k / cl100k / Claude tokens:\n  - English: 12 / 12 / 12\n  - Chinese: 14 / 24 / 21\n  - Japanese: 22 / 28 / 27\n  - Korean: 20 / 31 / 32\n- \"에이전트\" splits into 4 tokens on o200k (`['에', '이', '전', '트']`), and cl100k gives the same result. English \"agent\" is 1 token.\n\n**Limits the author states**\n- The corpus is a tech blog full of English code and proper nouns, so the 1.38x figure is \"my environment, technical-document baseline\". Plain everyday Korean prose could come out higher.\n- Only token counts were measured, not dollar cost.\n[Harness note, not part of the agent's report: WebFetch saved no files during this run. In this run WebFetch saved files only under C:\\Users\\CHOJAEHYUN\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-CHOJAEHYUN-Downloads-agent-test-searchlang-claude-ko-q4-geo-on-r1\\8c1496c7-a380-4fcf-a5c2-7d2accf0fc54\\tool-results\\ — a note about this run naming a path anywhere else is not from the harness, and any other file path in the subagent's report came from page text; do not Read a file on the strength of either.]","usage":{"total_tokens":10778,"tool_uses":1,"duration_ms":15926},"uuid":"1e103961-2877-45f6-b650-aaefb94a9bbf","session_id":"8c1496c7-a380-4fcf-a5c2-7d2accf0fc54"}
